  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Strong to severe wind gusts will be possible with \nthunderstorms this afternoon and evening, generally to the west of \nthe I-95 corridor.\n\nAn area of showers and thunderstorms has expanded in coverage early \nthis afternoon ahead of a cold front that is advancing from \nnorthwest to southeast. As of 2pm, the front is in Virginia, \ngenerally parallel to the VA/NC state border, but it appears that \nthe front should slow down through the afternoon and evening hours, \nfinally pushing south into North Carolina after midnight. In North \nCarolina, MLCAPE values are anywhere between 1000 and 2500 J/kg, \nwith the greatest values near the Triad. DCAPE values also have the \ngreatest values near the Triad, just above 1000 J/kg. Although wind \nshear should be minimal and will not do much to organize \nthunderstorms, the 12Z HREF is in fairly good agreement that a \ncluster of storms will develop across the Triad and move from north \nto south across the area. In fact, if the models are slightly behind \non their initialization, the storms that are currently across the \nTriad will likely be the cluster that moves to the south. The \ngreatest coverage is expected to extend as far east as the Triangle, \nalthough coverage should be more scattered farther east across Rocky \nMount and Goldsboro. The SPC marginal risk (level 1 of 5) generally \nextends from Interstate 95 to the west, with the greatest threat of \nsevere weather between 4 and 9pm. Damaging wind will be the threat. \nMost precipitation should come to an end after midnight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected on Monday and Tuesday. Locally excessive rainfall will be \npossible.\n\nAs a mid/upper trough develops over the Upper Midwest and Great \nLakes region early next week, a backdoor cold front will approach \ncentral NC from the north on Monday, passing through the region on \nTuesday. Ahead of the front, a warm and moist air mass will prevail, \nwith highs mostly in the upper-80s to lower-90s on Monday before \nbecoming cooler (within a few degrees of normal) for Tuesday. PW \nvalues will be around 2-2.25 inches (125-150% of normal) both days \nas SW flow aloft brings Gulf moisture into the Carolinas. This \ncombined with weak to moderate instability will result in scattered \nto numerous showers and thunderstorms. This will be beneficial for \nthe ongoing drought but could bring locally excessive rainfall with \nhourly amounts of 2+ inches possible where storms merge or train. \nThus WPC has the entire region in a marginal (level 1 of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall both Monday and Tuesday, with a slight (level 2 \nof 4) risk over our far north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A CAD pattern looks to set up from mid to late next \nweek, resulting in mostly cloudy skies, patchy light rain and \ndrizzle, and below-normal high temperatures.\n\nMid/upper troughing will strengthen and build into the Eastern US \nfrom Wednesday through Friday. Meanwhile, anomalously strong surface \nhigh pressure will nose down from SE Canada and northern New England \ninto the lee side of the Appalachians, as a surface low drifts north \nin the western Atlantic, though models differ substantially on its \nplacement. This will result in robust N/NE low-level flow and a \nlikely CAD pattern across the southern Mid-Atlantic. Current \nforecast highs are in the upper-60s to upper-70s, but some raw \nguidance such as the GFS is even cooler. Forecast lows are generally \nmid-50s to mid-60s. POPs are in the slight to low chance range, and \ngiven the stable CAD regime, precipitation is more likely to be of \nthe stratiform variety (patchy light rain and drizzle) as opposed to \nconvective.",
